The work package was approved yesterday. This morning, the access route has changed, another contractor is working nearby and overnight rain has altered conditions around the excavation.

The paperwork may still be complete. The question for the supervisor is whether the controls still match the work.

Metro and railway construction brings civil engineering, temporary works, lifting, electrical activities and contractor interfaces into a shared space. Where construction meets an operational railway, the consequences of a misunderstanding can extend beyond the worksite.

Critical risk management helps project teams concentrate on the events with the most serious potential consequences—and the controls that must work every time those exposures are present.

What is critical risk management in metro and railway construction?
Critical risk management in metro and railway construction identifies events that could cause fatal or life-changing harm, defines the controls needed to prevent or limit them, assigns accountable owners and verifies those controls at the worksite. Changes to conditions trigger review, with affected work paused where required controls cannot be confirmed.

Here, a critical control means a measure whose failure or absence would materially increase the likelihood or consequences of a serious event. A hazard list identifies what could cause harm. A critical-control approach also asks what prevents that event, how the measure can fail and what evidence shows it is effective.

Start with the serious event, then identify its controls
“Lifting risk” is too broad to guide a field decision. A clearer starting point is a defined event, such as a load falling into an occupied area or lifting equipment becoming unstable.

The project team can then consider prevention, consequence reduction and the circumstances that could weaken each control. Not every checklist item deserves the label “critical”. Prioritisation should reflect the event and the importance of the control, supported by competent technical judgement.

A bow-tie review can help organise threats, the loss-of-control event and consequences. Its value comes from the quality of the analysis and verification, not the appearance of the diagram.

Which critical risks deserve attention?
The following are examples for project planning, not a complete risk assessment or a substitute for engineered work methods.

Work interface	Potential serious event	Control questions for the responsible team
Work near operational rail	People or equipment exposed to train movements	Are the authorised work limits and protection arrangements understood, current and verified?
Electrical and buried services	Contact with energised equipment or service strike	Have competent authorised personnel confirmed the relevant boundaries and required precautions?
Excavations and shafts	Collapse, inundation or a fall into an opening	Do the approved support, access and inspection arrangements still suit current conditions?
Heavy lifting and segment installation	Dropped load, equipment instability or crushing	Does the approved plan reflect actual ground, equipment, load and work-area conditions?
Viaducts and temporary works	Fall from height or structural failure	Are the designed protection and support arrangements intact and used within their approved limits?
Construction traffic and public boundaries	Vehicle collision or public entry into a hazardous area	Are people, plant and public routes effectively separated as the layout changes?
Tunnels and enclosed work areas	Hazardous atmosphere, fire or impaired escape	Are specialist assessments, monitoring and emergency arrangements appropriate and verified?
Health exposures such as dust, noise and vibration also need systematic management. A focus on immediate fatal risks should not hide serious harm that develops over time.

Four areas where documented controls can lose effectiveness
1. The work changes without a corresponding review
A new access route, different equipment or revised sequence can invalidate earlier assumptions. Define the changes that require review and make them visible to the people authorising and carrying out the work.

A digital timestamp can show when a record was updated. It cannot establish that everyone affected understood the change.

2. Responsibility is split between organisations
The principal contractor, a specialist subcontractor and the rail operator may each own part of an arrangement. Problems arise when each assumes that another party has verified the interface.

Identify who provides the control, who checks it and who can authorise the next step. Use named responsibilities rather than “contractor to confirm”.

3. Verification checks documents instead of conditions
A signed plan establishes an approved intention. A field check establishes whether the relevant arrangement is present at the place and time of work.

Verification should be specific enough to identify failure. “Barrier satisfactory” provides less useful evidence than a check against the approved boundary, its condition and any changed access points.

4. A defect closes without a meaningful recheck
A photograph of a repaired barrier may confirm repair. It does not establish that the revised route is suitable or that another team will not remove it again.

Where findings recur, investigate the work arrangement and verify effectiveness under the conditions that caused the problem.

Build a practical critical-control record
For each selected serious-event scenario, keep a concise record that answers:

What must be prevented? State the event, activity and affected area.
Which controls are essential? Identify the measures and their purpose.
What does acceptable performance look like? Refer to the approved technical criteria and limits.
Who owns and verifies each control? Distinguish implementation, checking and authorisation.
When must it be checked? Include planned checks and relevant change triggers.
What evidence is required? Use suitable field records, competent inspection or other approved evidence.
What happens if it fails? Define escalation, the affected work restriction and authorised restart requirements.
Technical limits must come from competent engineering, the operator’s requirements and applicable rules. They should not be generated from a generic AI response.

