A practical example: the access route changes before a planned lift
Imagine a rail-construction project where a temporary delivery route is revised shortly before a scheduled lift. The new route passes through an area that the lifting team had intended to keep clear.

The useful response is to identify the conflict before the work proceeds. The responsible teams review the route, lifting arrangement and affected work, then confirm the revised controls and brief those involved. A software record can preserve the change, assigned actions and verification evidence.

When the conflict is resolved, restart follows the project’s authorisation process. Closing a digital action is not, by itself, permission to resume.

This is an illustrative scenario, not a customer case study or a claim of measured NeoEHS results.

Use technical guidance with the correct scope
UK HSE guidance on lifting operations emphasises competent planning and supervision. Its excavation guidance addresses ground conditions, services and competent inspection, while its temporary-works guidance explains the need to plan and manage engineered support and access arrangements.

The UK Office of Rail and Road’s electrical safety guidance highlights the danger of equipment believed to be dead that remains live. These references inform the discussion; they are not a universal legal specification. Each project must apply its jurisdiction’s requirements, operator rules and approved engineering procedures.

Measure control performance rather than paperwork volume
A practical review can track failed critical-control checks, overdue verification, time from failure to acknowledgement, repeated defects and changes awaiting reassessment. Review these alongside the work exposure and inspection coverage.

Define the denominator and period. Ten failed checks among a thousand verified controls means something different from ten failures where only a small sample was checked. A rising count may also reflect better reporting.

Keep severe failures visible individually. Averaging them into a reassuring overall score can obscure the decision a supervisor needs to make.

Frequently asked questions
How is critical risk management different from a general risk assessment?
A general assessment identifies and evaluates hazards. Critical risk management adds focused ownership and verification of the controls that prevent or limit the most serious events. It complements the wider assessment.

What makes a control critical?
Its failure or absence would materially increase the likelihood or consequences of a defined serious event. The decision requires competent analysis of the scenario and its controls.

Can NeoEHS authorise work on an operational railway?
NeoEHS can support configured records and workflows. Railway access, protection, isolation and work authorisation remain subject to the operator’s approved arrangements and authorised personnel.

When should controls be reviewed?
Use the project’s required review schedule and change triggers, including altered conditions, equipment, sequences, work boundaries, reported failures or relevant incidents.

Can AI confirm that a critical control is effective?
AI can support evidence review and selected monitoring. It cannot replace the required field verification, engineering assurance or authorised decisions.

Where should a project team start?
Choose one significant activity, define its serious-event scenarios and essential controls, assign owners and agree verification evidence. Test the workflow with the people who perform and authorise the work before expanding it.

